#  Access Modifiers

## What are Access Modifiers


Access modifiers define the visibility and accessibility of classes, variables, methods, and constructors.



##  Types of Access Modifiers

1. `public`
2. `private`
3. `protected`
4. `default` (no keyword)



# 1. public

### Access Level

* Accessible from anywhere

### Example

public class Test {
    public int x = 10;
}

--the class and the variable we can access from everywhere


#  2. private

### Access Level

Accessible only within the same class

###  Example

class Test {
    private int x = 10;
}


--Used in Encapsulation




#  3. protected

###  Access Level

* Same package
* Different package via inheritance

### Example

class Test {
    protected int x = 10;
}


#  4. default (no modifier)

###  Access Level

* Accessible only within same package

### Example
class Test {
    int x = 10; // default
}



# Access Summary Table

| Modifier  | Same Class | Same Package | Different Package  | Subclass (Different Package)  |
| --------- | ---------- | ------------ | -----------------  | ----------------------------  |
| public    | ✅          | ✅            | ✅              | ✅                            |
| protected | ✅          | ✅            | ❌              | ✅                            |
| default   | ✅          | ✅            | ❌              | ❌                            |
| private   | ✅          | ❌            | ❌              | ❌                            |



# Access Modifiers with Classes

* Only `public` and `default` allowed for classes


## ✔ Valid

public class A {}
class B {}


## ❌ Invalid

private class A {}   // not allowed
protected class A {} // not allowed



# Why Access Modifiers are Important

* Data hiding
* Security
* Controlled access
* Maintainable code



# Best Practices

* Use `private` for variables
* Use `public` getters/setters
* Use `protected` for inheritance
* Use `default` for package-level access
